WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.276 --> 00:00:03.017
- There are a lot of people

00:00:03.017 --> 00:00:06.017
who are 45 to 60 years old

00:00:06.017 --> 00:00:08.331
who just are complaining about millennials

00:00:08.331 --> 00:00:09.682
and they're not actually asking

00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:11.946
how they are getting back in the mix

00:00:11.946 --> 00:00:13.914
by becoming a beginner again.

00:00:13.914 --> 00:00:15.853
- And are being held accountable

00:00:15.853 --> 00:00:19.436
for parenting and
creating the millennials.

00:00:20.718 --> 00:00:22.285
- You got your perspective.

00:00:22.285 --> 00:00:23.865
(upbeat music)

00:00:23.865 --> 00:00:25.593
(crowd cheering)

00:00:25.593 --> 00:00:26.426
I just wanna be happy,

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:28.676
don't you want to be happy?

00:00:31.004 --> 00:00:31.837
Hey everybody.

00:00:31.837 --> 00:00:32.882
It's Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:35.475
and I have a really special guest here.

00:00:35.475 --> 00:00:38.487
An entrepreneur that I've
known for a very long time

00:00:38.487 --> 00:00:39.523
and I got an email.

00:00:39.523 --> 00:00:40.424
I got a lot of emails,

00:00:40.424 --> 00:00:42.292
I got an email either from him or his crew

00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:45.493
and they're like, "Chips got
some stuff to talk about."

00:00:45.493 --> 00:00:46.617
I'm like, "yes!"

00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:47.470
So here we are.
- [Chip] Thank you.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.604
On MLK day, we're filming
and recording this.

00:00:49.604 --> 00:00:52.140
Chip Conley is in the building.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.024
Chip, why don't you tell everybody

00:00:54.024 --> 00:00:56.139
who's listening a little
bit about yourself?

00:00:56.139 --> 00:00:57.262
And then, very frankly,

00:00:57.262 --> 00:00:58.753
some of the things you want to talk about

00:00:58.753 --> 00:00:59.586
and then we'll riff.

00:00:59.586 --> 00:01:00.419
- Yeah.

00:01:00.419 --> 00:01:01.448
So, great to be here.

00:01:01.448 --> 00:01:02.580
Congratulations on the digs here

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:03.446
- [Gary] Thanks. Thank you.

00:01:03.446 --> 00:01:04.798
It's pretty nice.

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:06.711
I'm staying at the Equinox
Hotel right near by,

00:01:06.711 --> 00:01:08.160
- [Gary] Which is very nice.
- Which is very, very nice.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.606
Getting a massage tonight.

00:01:09.606 --> 00:01:10.574
(chuckles)

00:01:10.574 --> 00:01:11.407
Um, so,

00:01:11.407 --> 00:01:12.240
yeah.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.253
I started the second largest
boutique hotel company

00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:16.495
in the U.S. when I was
26, called Joie de Vivre,

00:01:16.495 --> 00:01:17.950
based in San Francisco.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.622
All 52 of the hotels
were all in California.

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:23.311
Sold it and then seven years--

00:01:23.311 --> 00:01:24.471
- How did the first one start?

00:01:24.471 --> 00:01:25.535
'Cause I know this audience.

00:01:25.535 --> 00:01:26.934
When they hear that hyperbole,

00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:28.504
everyone's like "how?"

00:01:28.504 --> 00:01:32.080
- I was two years out of
Stanford Business School,

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.083
in fact I was hanging out

00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:34.345
with my friend Seth Godin yesterday,

00:01:34.345 --> 00:01:35.544
here in New York,

00:01:35.544 --> 00:01:38.155
and who I went to business school with.

00:01:38.155 --> 00:01:39.769
So, I started the first one

00:01:39.769 --> 00:01:42.026
by buying a pay-by-the-hour motel.

00:01:42.026 --> 00:01:43.855
Not because I was frequenting it--

00:01:43.855 --> 00:01:45.145
(both men laugh)

00:01:45.145 --> 00:01:46.259
- [Gary] Which would've been fun!

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:48.014
- Because it was cheap and--

00:01:48.014 --> 00:01:49.605
- How'd you have any capital?

00:01:49.605 --> 00:01:51.868
- I raised about 1.1 million dollars.

00:01:51.868 --> 00:01:52.853
So, I was 26.

00:01:52.853 --> 00:01:54.605
I had gone to Stanford Business School,

00:01:54.605 --> 00:01:55.438
gone to Stanford undergrad.

00:01:55.438 --> 00:01:56.585
Had a lot of friends who had money

00:01:56.585 --> 00:01:59.880
I was not from a money family.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:00.929
- What year was this?

00:02:00.929 --> 00:02:03.555
- It was 1986, '86, '87.

00:02:03.555 --> 00:02:05.096
- You know it's funny.

00:02:05.096 --> 00:02:06.553
That's a little bit, like I don't have--

00:02:06.553 --> 00:02:07.403
- [Chip] Before your time.

00:02:07.403 --> 00:02:08.236
- Yes.

00:02:08.236 --> 00:02:09.069
Enough before my time

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:10.763
that I don't have a good enough read

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.935
on how ridiculously hard

00:02:12.935 --> 00:02:14.110
or ridiculously easy.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.302
Even going to Stanford,

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:17.582
when I hear '86, '87,

00:02:17.582 --> 00:02:19.440
and I hear 1.1,

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.481
like, I'm like oh,
that's a real accomplish-

00:02:21.481 --> 00:02:22.672
You know now, kids--

00:02:22.672 --> 00:02:23.505
- [Chip] I don't know

00:02:23.505 --> 00:02:24.553
- Well, I'll say this.

00:02:24.553 --> 00:02:26.514
Listen, raising capital is
it's own game in itself.

00:02:26.514 --> 00:02:30.486
But, today, you don't
have to be that remarkable

00:02:30.486 --> 00:02:32.236
to actually be able to
raise a million dollars

00:02:32.236 --> 00:02:33.069
for a start-up idea

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:34.296
because of what's happened
in the last decade.

00:02:34.296 --> 00:02:35.321
- [Chip] That's true.

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.504
- Paint this for me.

00:02:36.504 --> 00:02:39.521
In 1986, '87, in the Stanford world,

00:02:39.521 --> 00:02:42.414
like, was that incredibly difficult?

00:02:42.414 --> 00:02:45.521
Was that two families
that had so much money?

00:02:45.521 --> 00:02:47.070
Give me a little context
to make it practical.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.537
- It was about 20 investors,

00:02:48.537 --> 00:02:50.513
all putting about 50k each in.

00:02:50.513 --> 00:02:52.411
So, it was like a syndication,

00:02:52.411 --> 00:02:54.335
that's what we used to call it.

00:02:54.335 --> 00:02:55.482
- Did people think it was crazy

00:02:55.482 --> 00:02:56.315
to give a 26-year-old?
- It was crazy

00:02:56.315 --> 00:02:58.441
because I was 26. And I
had no hotel experience.

00:02:58.441 --> 00:03:00.807
- Again, now, the 22-year-old
getting a million bucks

00:03:00.807 --> 00:03:01.817
seems common.

00:03:01.817 --> 00:03:03.344
Then it seems to me like an enigma.

00:03:03.344 --> 00:03:05.097
- It was wacky and it was a--

00:03:05.097 --> 00:03:07.876
- Or was things happening
because it was Stanford,

00:03:07.876 --> 00:03:08.709
and San Francisco.

00:03:08.709 --> 00:03:10.448
- San Francisco was-
this was - San Francisco

00:03:10.448 --> 00:03:12.517
was not what it is today, then.

00:03:12.517 --> 00:03:14.253
Long story short is, it
was big people betting

00:03:14.253 --> 00:03:15.578
on me, not on the business plan.

00:03:15.578 --> 00:03:16.814
And not on the real estate because

00:03:16.814 --> 00:03:18.602
it was a broken down motel in the

00:03:18.602 --> 00:03:21.235
Tenderloin of San Francisco.

00:03:21.235 --> 00:03:23.355
And it became- So, I raised the money.

00:03:23.355 --> 00:03:25.353
Took me three and a half years to

00:03:25.353 --> 00:03:26.849
get it to a place where it was

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:27.682
clearly a success story.

00:03:27.682 --> 00:03:29.197
It became the rock n' roll hotel

00:03:29.197 --> 00:03:30.030
in San Francisco.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:30.984
So everybody from David Bowie to

00:03:30.984 --> 00:03:34.052
Linda Ronstadt were staying there

00:03:34.052 --> 00:03:36.045
as this funky motel and--

00:03:36.045 --> 00:03:37.581
- How did you get the first "get"?

00:03:37.581 --> 00:03:38.556
- The first "get"?

00:03:38.556 --> 00:03:40.268
- Yeah, like the first
person that was cool,

00:03:40.268 --> 00:03:43.101
that you got, because
that became the domino

00:03:43.101 --> 00:03:44.484
to--
- In terms of the investors

00:03:44.484 --> 00:03:45.702
- No,
- or in terms of the guests?

00:03:45.702 --> 00:03:46.535
- Guests.

00:03:46.535 --> 00:03:48.328
- It was all about the tour manager.

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:50.028
Everybody thought it
was all about going out

00:03:50.028 --> 00:03:52.946
and marketing to the travel agents.

00:03:52.946 --> 00:03:54.768
No, it was actually the
person who made the decision,

00:03:54.768 --> 00:03:56.251
was the guy who's about
five years older than

00:03:56.251 --> 00:03:57.702
the rest of the band, who wanted to make

00:03:57.702 --> 00:03:58.958
sure they didn't do an overdose or that

00:03:58.958 --> 00:04:00.310
a groupie wouldn't, like, hijack them.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.143
- [Gary] Yes.

00:04:01.143 --> 00:04:03.470
- And so we gave that- We
did a deal where we said,

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:06.106
"We have a massage
treatment room, you can get

00:04:06.106 --> 00:04:09.334
a free massage if you
bring us ten room nights."

00:04:09.334 --> 00:04:11.174
and so the tour managers
started talking to

00:04:11.174 --> 00:04:13.127
each other, and next thing I knew,

00:04:13.127 --> 00:04:14.484
all the tour managers and the bus

00:04:14.484 --> 00:04:15.764
drivers- the bus drivers
liked us just because

00:04:15.764 --> 00:04:17.298
we had free bus parking too.

00:04:17.298 --> 00:04:18.663
So at the end of the day, you 'gotta

00:04:18.663 --> 00:04:20.269
determine, like, what do you have that's

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:21.107
your benefit?

00:04:21.107 --> 00:04:22.276
And we had a massage studio and

00:04:22.276 --> 00:04:23.492
a parking lot.

00:04:23.492 --> 00:04:25.288
- Uh-huh. No, I like that.

00:04:25.288 --> 00:04:27.204
- And the tour manager bought it,

00:04:27.204 --> 00:04:29.485
and the next thing I knew-
we basically for thirty-three

00:04:29.485 --> 00:04:30.541
years now that hotel,

00:04:30.541 --> 00:04:31.828
it's called The Phoenix.

00:04:31.828 --> 00:04:34.328
Funky motel in the Tenderloin.

00:04:35.459 --> 00:04:36.987
- And were you there every day?

00:04:36.987 --> 00:04:38.302
- I was there every day in the early days.

00:04:38.302 --> 00:04:39.233
Yeah, for sure.,
- [Gary] Yeah, yeah of course.

00:04:39.233 --> 00:04:40.066
- And I was learning the business.

00:04:40.066 --> 00:04:41.030
I didn't know anything about it.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:41.863
- [Gary] Of course.

00:04:41.863 --> 00:04:43.038
- I didn't go to Cornell Hotel School

00:04:43.038 --> 00:04:44.798
or anything like that. So--

00:04:44.798 --> 00:04:46.205
- Why'd you want to do it?

00:04:46.205 --> 00:04:48.016
- I loved- I first of all had a lot of

00:04:48.016 --> 00:04:48.949
people staying on my couch.

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:50.096
(both men laugh)

00:04:50.096 --> 00:04:52.186
- So you know, little market research

00:04:52.186 --> 00:04:54.204
was like, "why are you
all staying on my couch?

00:04:54.204 --> 00:04:55.699
Why don't you stay at a hotel?"

00:04:55.699 --> 00:04:57.210
And they kept saying, "the hotels in San

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.324
Francisco are either really expensive

00:04:59.324 --> 00:05:01.027
or really boring."

00:05:01.027 --> 00:05:02.228
- Who are these people?

00:05:02.228 --> 00:05:04.578
- Friends, who are coming
to visit San Francisco.

00:05:04.578 --> 00:05:06.155
So I--
- Where did you grow up?

00:05:06.155 --> 00:05:07.778
- I grew up in L.A. Grew up in Long Beach.

00:05:07.778 --> 00:05:09.144
I went to Snoop Dogg's high school.

00:05:09.144 --> 00:05:10.679
So I'm from LBC- famous.

00:05:10.679 --> 00:05:11.512
- I love it.

00:05:11.512 --> 00:05:13.178
- Long Beach Poly High School,

00:05:13.178 --> 00:05:14.488
was number one high school for--

00:05:14.488 --> 00:05:15.388
- Might have been a slightly different

00:05:15.388 --> 00:05:16.888
high school when you and
Snoop were there or same?

00:05:16.888 --> 00:05:17.821
- [Chip] No, no.

00:05:17.821 --> 00:05:18.654
- Yeah, I'm askin'.

00:05:18.654 --> 00:05:19.645
Snoop was about ten years younger than me,

00:05:19.645 --> 00:05:20.975
but he stayed at my hotels.

00:05:20.975 --> 00:05:22.708
Which you need to know after Snoop

00:05:22.708 --> 00:05:23.997
stays in your hotel
room, you definitely need

00:05:23.997 --> 00:05:24.830
to fumigate a little bit.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:25.663
- Yep.

00:05:25.663 --> 00:05:26.891
- Just because there's a little bit

00:05:26.891 --> 00:05:29.141
still a stench of the weed.

00:05:30.326 --> 00:05:32.176
So yeah, when I was back there,

00:05:32.176 --> 00:05:34.757
no- I heard they called
me "curious white boy".

00:05:34.757 --> 00:05:37.111
I did basketball, uh, like you.

00:05:37.111 --> 00:05:37.944
- [Gary] Nice.

00:05:37.944 --> 00:05:39.477
- Last time I think I saw Gary

00:05:39.477 --> 00:05:40.710
as we were playing.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.505
We were at summit, at
sea, up on the top deck,

00:05:43.505 --> 00:05:45.918
playing full court basketball while he

00:05:45.918 --> 00:05:47.690
was in his sandals.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:48.973
I'm surprised you didn't break a leg.

00:05:48.973 --> 00:05:50.107
- [Gary] It's true, I'm super happy.

00:05:50.107 --> 00:05:52.432
- No, but I had that company
for twenty-four years,

00:05:52.432 --> 00:05:53.315
sold it.

00:05:53.315 --> 00:05:54.946
It's now a high brand

00:05:54.946 --> 00:05:56.506
and long story short is, I,

00:05:56.506 --> 00:05:58.065
seven years ago, was asked by the

00:05:58.065 --> 00:05:59.484
three founders of Airbnb to come help

00:05:59.484 --> 00:06:01.529
them grow their company into a

00:06:01.529 --> 00:06:03.022
global hospitality brand.

00:06:03.022 --> 00:06:04.300
So, I've spent seven years as

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.457
Brian Chesky's mentor and I was the head

00:06:07.457 --> 00:06:09.042
of global hospitality and strategy

00:06:09.042 --> 00:06:11.000
for the company for four years full time.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.208
And then for the last
three years, just been

00:06:12.208 --> 00:06:13.828
like a strategic advisor to them.

00:06:13.828 --> 00:06:14.995
- That's cool.

00:06:16.269 --> 00:06:17.310
- And part of the reason I'm here

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:20.421
is because, while I was there, you know,

00:06:20.421 --> 00:06:21.998
they call me "curious white boy"

00:06:21.998 --> 00:06:23.252
when I was in high school.

00:06:23.252 --> 00:06:25.477
They call me the "modern elder" at Airbnb,

00:06:25.477 --> 00:06:26.895
and I did not like that.

00:06:26.895 --> 00:06:28.881
But I was twice the age of the

00:06:28.881 --> 00:06:30.163
average employee at Airbnb.

00:06:30.163 --> 00:06:31.491
I started when I was fifty-two,

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:32.324
I'm fifty-nine now.

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:33.528
- For everybody who's listening,

00:06:33.528 --> 00:06:35.970
my great run in kind of

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:37.744
the Silicon Valley investment world,

00:06:37.744 --> 00:06:42.744
definitely was kind of like
'05-- to like '06 to like 2010,

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:44.845
and what's interesting about hearing this

00:06:44.845 --> 00:06:48.010
story is that Chip absolutely like was,

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:50.259
in those circles with me

00:06:50.259 --> 00:06:52.041
and there was just like--
there was a bunch--

00:06:52.041 --> 00:06:54.903
there was probably like forty
to a hundred fifty people

00:06:54.903 --> 00:06:57.756
that weren't hardcore attack.

00:06:57.756 --> 00:06:59.862
Had come from different backgrounds,

00:06:59.862 --> 00:07:01.721
but were kind of like in the mix

00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:03.640
one way or the other.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.438
It's really fun to hear that,

00:07:05.438 --> 00:07:06.362
that's where you ended up

00:07:06.362 --> 00:07:08.672
and how it makes so much fucking sense

00:07:08.672 --> 00:07:11.399
because the value prop on context

00:07:11.399 --> 00:07:12.373
and reps that you were able to

00:07:12.373 --> 00:07:14.201
bring that exact company is like

00:07:14.201 --> 00:07:16.226
the most logical shit I've ever heard.

00:07:16.226 --> 00:07:19.225
- It was so funny because when I joined--

00:07:19.225 --> 00:07:20.550
first of all for the first five months

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:21.954
we didn't tell anyone that I joined.

00:07:21.954 --> 00:07:23.249
So, we didn't tell the press.

00:07:23.249 --> 00:07:24.919
We wanted to see how
Brian and I got along.

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:26.640
- Where did you meet Brian originally?

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:27.531
- Brian just called me.

00:07:27.531 --> 00:07:29.281
He just called me out of the blue.

00:07:30.202 --> 00:07:33.016
One of the things I'll say about Brian,

00:07:33.016 --> 00:07:34.475
a lot of people think it's the hubris

00:07:34.475 --> 00:07:35.736
that makes a great CEO, its actually

00:07:35.736 --> 00:07:36.569
the humility.

00:07:36.569 --> 00:07:37.464
- Hundred percent.

00:07:37.464 --> 00:07:40.226
- In Brian's case he
had the humility to say

00:07:40.226 --> 00:07:42.760
"I don't know a bunch of
stuff, so I am gonna go out--

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:43.993
and when it comes to hospitality,

00:07:43.993 --> 00:07:45.773
I'm gonna go talk to this guy who's in

00:07:45.773 --> 00:07:46.606
San Francisco.

00:07:46.606 --> 00:07:48.367
Who's created a boutique hotel company,

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:49.607
he's an entrepreneur."

00:07:49.607 --> 00:07:51.257
- Who's in the mix with our community.

00:07:51.257 --> 00:07:52.635
Which really mattered at the time.

00:07:52.635 --> 00:07:53.468
- [Chip] That's true.

00:07:53.468 --> 00:07:56.639
- It always does but I
remembered very vividly.

00:07:56.639 --> 00:07:57.591
- All the people who looked at me like,

00:07:57.591 --> 00:07:58.424
"what the hell are you doing?"

00:07:58.424 --> 00:08:00.126
finally was announced five months later,

00:08:00.126 --> 00:08:02.314
it was like why would you --

00:08:02.314 --> 00:08:03.408
- Well that was your old world.

00:08:03.408 --> 00:08:04.542
- My brethren in the hotel world

00:08:04.542 --> 00:08:07.077
had no clue what Airbnb was.

00:08:07.077 --> 00:08:09.410
And so, long story short is,

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.050
it led me to becoming this "modern elder".

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.803
And I'm what the hell's a "modern elder"?

00:08:14.803 --> 00:08:17.120
The traditional elder of the past

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:18.240
was all about reverence.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:19.531
You revered your elders,

00:08:19.531 --> 00:08:22.346
but a modern elder is about relevance.

